===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[amount]] of [[space]] between two [[point]]s, usually geographical points, usually (but not necessarily) measured along a [[straight]] line.
#: {{ux|en|The '''distance''' to Petersborough is thirty miles.}}
#: {{ux|en|From Moscow, the '''distance''' is relatively short to Saint Petersburg, relatively long to Novosibirsk, but even greater to Vladivostok.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=5|passage=Then everybody once more knelt, and soon the blessing was pronounced. The choir and the clergy trooped out slowly,{{nb...}}, down the nave to the western door.{{...}}At a seemingly immense '''distance''' the surpliced group stopped to say the last prayer.}}
# Length or interval of time.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1718|author=w:Matthew Prior|title=Preface to a Collection of Poems|passage=ten years' '''distance''' between my writing the one and the other}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1795|author=w:John Playfair|title=Elements of Geometry
|passage=the writings of Euclid at the '''distance''' of two thousand years}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} The [[difference]]; the subjective [[measure]] between two quantities.
#: {{ux|en|We're narrowing the '''distance''' between the two versions of the bill.&emsp; {{nowrap|The '''distance''' between the lowest and next gear on my bicycle is annoying.}}}}
# Remoteness of place; a remote place.
#* {{RQ:Irving Sketch Book|title=Rip Van Winkle|page=71|passage=As he was about to descend, he heard a voice from a '''distance''', hallooing, "Rip Van Winkle! Rip Van Winkle!"}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1799|author={{w|Thomas Campbell (poet)|Thomas Campbell}}|title=The Pleasure of Hope|passage='Tis '''distance''' lends enchantment to the view.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Cato|5|1|passage=[He] waits at '''distance''' till he hears from Cato.}}
# Remoteness in succession or relation.
#: {{ux|en|the '''distance''' between a descendant and his ancestor}}
# A space marked out in the last part of a [[racecourse]].
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|passage=the horse that ran the whole field out of '''distance'''}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|figuratively}} The entire [[amount]] of [[progress]] to an objective.
#: {{ux|en|He had promised to perform this task, but did not go the '''distance'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|figuratively}} A withholding of intimacy; alienation; variance.
#: {{ux|en|The friendship did not survive the row: they kept each other at a '''distance'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Seditions and Troubles|passage=Setting them [factions] at '''distance''', or at least distrust amongst themselves.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=8|passage=On the part of Heaven, / Now alienated, '''distance''' and distaste.|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|III|passage=In former days every tavern of repute kept such a room for its own select circle, a club, or society, of habitués, who met every evening, for a pipe and a cheerful glass.{{...}}Strangers might enter the room, but they were made to feel that they were there on sufferance: they were received with '''distance''' and suspicion.}}
# The remoteness or reserve which respect requires; hence, respect; ceremoniousness.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Indian Emperour|page=4|passage=though you ſee / The King is kind, I hope your modeſty / VVill knovv, what '''diſtance''' to the Crovvn is due.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1706|author=w:Francis Atterbury|title=A Sermon Preached in the Guild-Hall Chapel, September 28 1706|passage=’Tis by respect and '''distance''' that authority is upheld.}}
# The space measured back from the winning-post which a [[racehorse]] running in a [[heat]] must reach when the winner has covered the whole course, in order to run in the final heat.

